{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791597342,"nonce":"GFiaZ95ca9tPMPhve6HlmIp8wyM=","title":"sd_notch: flares joined with smin, bulge and peak reach only min(8, half the body) into the body","message":"Reported by ashui-56 from ashui and blinc_ts, whose notch distance function shares Blinc's construction and carries the fixes. Read from common/sdf_common.wgsl `sd_notch`; not rendered in Blinc. Queued, not started.\n\n1. Flares. The four concave-corner flares are joined with `d = smin(d, flare, smin_k)` (k = 1.5). A flare's top edge lies along the body's edge, so smin bulges the shared edge by k/4 and leaves a step where the flare rect ends inside the body. Fills hardly show it; liquid glass refracts along the distance gradient and shows a bright sliver at the flare's inner end. Fix: `d = min(d, flare)` for all four (the flare's ellipse meets the body's side tangentially). Correction from the reporter: a flare rect must reach at most 8px into the body, because a deeper rect still bends the gradient at its far end. Blinc's flare boxes already use `join_depth = min(8, half the body's smaller side)`, so only the join changes here. Refs: ashui ed1a090 and 2dd46ab (final), ashui issue dd90c35, reproducer ashui tools/snapshot/scenes/NotchGlass.hx (concave-top dropdown with liquid glass).\n\n2. Bulge and peak reach. Both use `join_depth` (at most 8px), so an inset shadow ring wider than 8px shows a straight seam where the piece meets the body. Fix: only these two reach `min(inner_size.x, inner_size.y) * 0.5`, clipped to the piece's own width so a deep piece does not square off rounded corners: for the bulge `max(max(disk_sd, p.y - base_y - into), abs(p.x - cx) - half_w)`; for the peak `max(sd_triangle(...), abs(p.x - cx) - half_w)`. Refs: ashui 7bb5a15 and 2dd46ab, blinc_abi issue 3a35de9, reproducer ashui tools/snapshot/scenes/NotchInset.hx.\n\n3. Inset shadows on a notched box. blinc_abi's display list built them on the padding box without the notch, so the ring followed the nominal rectangle; its fix (d57246c) draws each inset shadow on the whole box with the notch, spread grown by the widest border side, then draws the border again over the shadows. In Blinc, paint/basic.rs paints inner shadows on the rounded-rect box with a padding-box clip; whether a notched element reaches that path, or paints inset shadows at all, was not checked.\n\nNotch fills and borders are unchanged apart from about 200 antialiasing pixels in the reporter's snapshots.","files":null}]}